nodecode-system-one is a rewrite of NMTZ-z's NMTZ-z/system-one-benchmark-lab:

  https://github.com/NMTZ-z/system-one-benchmark-lab
  commit 1095495019b2dff2b4231ed70ec23a3ea716e2f6
  Apache-2.0. The repository's LICENSE keeps the Apache-2.0 placeholder for the
  holder line: Copyright [yyyy] [name of copyright owner] (LICENSE).

The rewritten plugin is integrations/hermes/local-system-one-hermes at that
commit: three gates of a local decision service, asked by hooks on a Hermes turn.
Its wire is carried over unchanged: POST /v1/workflows/search-gate and
POST /v1/workflows/model-tier-gate with {task, context, request_id} -- the task
bounded to 4000 characters and the context to 2000 -- and POST
/v1/workflows/notification-gate with {event (6000 characters), context, urgency,
user_action_required, blocking_failure, routine_update, deadline_minutes,
request_id}; the response fields decision, tier, delivery, decision_source,
reason, probability_search, probability_strong, priority_score, confidence and
backend; the 500 ms per-gate ceiling, clamped to 50 ms..5 s; the fail-open rule
that any error, timeout or incomplete answer leaves the caller's own choice
standing; the audited hard no-Web reasons (bounded_transform_task,
connected_app_data, local_file_or_repo) and hard-fast reasons
(bounded_transform, bounded_structured_transform) that the original's Canary mode
acted on; the loopback default endpoint; and the three independently switched
gates. LICENSE is the repository's LICENSE, unchanged.

What was left out, and why: the Hermes hook points themselves
(pre_llm_call, post_llm_call, on_session_end) and the llm_request middleware,
because Nodecode's one tool is eval and a cell's vocabulary is Lisp functions,
so the gates are verbs plus two tools (system_one_route, system_one_notify) and
the /system-one command; the Canary request mutations, because no provider
request seam is a cell's to edit here, so an audited rule is said as advice on
the decision line instead of applied; the owned-kanban-card and cron-wrapper
task enrichment, because it reads Hermes's own database and environment; the
per-turn off/shadow/canary mode and the persisted shadow history, because a
cell is asked rather than hooked and its answers are never persisted; and
Python's urllib, replaced by the image's own nlk:http. One addition: an optional
bearer token (auth_token or auth_token_env) for a sidecar started with one,
which the original never sent.
